  "notes": "The strictest honky-tonk traditionalist to hold 90s radio. The one parameter the entry calls its most useful is the bimodal tempo distribution — \"a ballad mass at 60–96 and a fast group at 140–172 with almost nothing between 100 and 139\" — so `bpm` spans 60–172 with `mode` at 168, inside the fast group the entry calls \"the section's clearest fast train beat\"; the ballad mass is reached through the parent genre's inherited `ballad` mood. Drums: \"straight 8th snare stream, sticks, two-beat kick, hat on quarters\", and the kit \"mixed correspondingly small and dry — model low room, no gated reverb\", which is why `velocityTiers` are the quietest of the stick-wing models here (accent 96–116) and `percs` is two lanes at −9 dB. Harmony is the stated I–IV–V with V7 dominant throughout, plus the weepers' ii–V and the IV-chord dwell; the sevenths live in `extensions`, not in the numerals. The melody follows \"low-to-mid baritone, narrow range, heavy use of the flatted third as a passing ornament; phrases end on long held notes\" — a low register, a high step share, `halfStepDissonance` raised for the flat third and `articulation` legato. Arrangement is the entry's \"long instrumental (steel then fiddle, 8+8)\", i.e. a 16-bar instrumental section rather than a 4-bar solo. ⚠ INFERRED: velocity tiers, the −2 ms snare offset, `percs` contents, and all melody, countermelody and bassline numbers — the entry gives adjectives (\"small and dry\", \"narrow range\") and these are this author's reading of them. ⚠ The tempo readings carry [LOW-CONF] as a class and the entry states \"harmony generalised [LOW-CONF]\", so `confidence` is medium against a medium-high rating.",
